<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:vm="using:Hoard.App.ViewModels"
             xmlns:views="using:Hoard.App.Views"
             x:Class="Hoard.App.Views.FeedView"
             x:DataType="vm:FeedViewModel">

    <!--
        ══ The Feed (M8) ════════════════════════════════════════════════════
        The screen the app opens on, and the point of the product: five shelves
        over one scoring pass, each shelf stating its own reason and each card
        stating its own.

        WHY RAILS AND NOT A WALL. The library already IS the wall, one rail
        click away, and it is the right shape for six hundred covers you are
        scanning. This surface is the opposite errand: five short arguments, in
        order, each of which has to be READ rather than scanned. So the shelves
        stack vertically and the games run horizontally inside them — the shape
        that puts a heading, a pitch and a row of reasons on one scanline
        (§6a: "a shelf is a REASON with items attached").

        WHY THERE IS NO VIRTUALIZATION HERE, and it is measured rather than
        assumed. The engine caps a shelf at ten items and omits empty shelves,
        so the ceiling is fifty cards — against the six hundred that made
        CoverWall necessary. CoverWall is also the wrong tool twice over: it is
        vertical, and it is uniform, and these cards are neither (a rail's height
        is set by its longest reason). Fifty cards of two images each is the same
        order as one screenful of the wall, which the app already realizes on
        every scroll. If a shelf cap ever moves past ~40, measure again.

        WHAT THE PATCHED SHELF DOES NOT GET. It is the app's headline story and
        therefore the most tempting place in the interface to spend Flare — on
        its heading, on its cards' edges, on its count. It gets none of it. Every
        shelf is drawn identically and the patched one leads by being first,
        which is the engine's claim order. Flare's one appearance on this screen
        is the unread dot on a cover, which is what it has always meant (§2).
    -->

    <UserControl.Styles>

        <!-- The shelf heading. Bricolage, because a shelf title is the app's own
             voice making a claim — but not display-l, which is the screen's own
             name and has to outrank it. -->
        <Style Selector="TextBlock.shelf">
            <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource DisplayFont}"/>
            <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Bold"/>
            <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="17"/>
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource Text}"/>
        </Style>

        <!-- The rail's own scrollbar is a divider of ours, not the window's, so
             it keeps its flush position rather than taking §9.1's edge inset —
             the same opt-out the rail and the detail modal take. -->
        <Style Selector="ScrollViewer.rail /template/ ScrollBar">
            <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0"/>
        </Style>

    </UserControl.Styles>

    <Grid RowDefinitions="Auto,*" Background="{StaticResource PaneGround}">

        <!-- ══ Header ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
             Same construction as the Stores screen's: the name, one line of
             what this is, and a rule under it. The count is a NUMBER, so it
             sets in Plex Mono with tabular figures like every number in the
             app — and it is only stated once there is one to state, because "0
             games scored" while the pass is out would be a lie the screen tells
             for half a second. -->
        <Border Grid.Row="0"
                Padding="24,20,24,18"
                BorderBrush="{StaticResource Line}"
                BorderThickness="0,0,0,1">
            <StackPanel Spacing="7">
                <TextBlock Classes="display-l" Text="{Binding Title}"/>

                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6"
                            IsVisible="{Binding HasCandidates}">
                    <TextBlock Classes="data"
                               Foreground="{StaticResource Text}"
                               Text="{Binding CandidateCountText}"/>
                    <TextBlock Classes="body"
                               Foreground="{StaticResource TextDim}"
                               Text="games scored today, and every card says why it's on it."/>
                </StackPanel>

                <!-- §6's confidence copy, driven by the tier the engine reports.
                     It goes quiet once the library has enough history that
                     saying anything would be an apology for nothing. -->
                <TextBlock Classes="body" FontSize="12"
                           MaxWidth="640"
                           HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                           TextWrapping="Wrap"
                           LineHeight="18"
                           Foreground="{StaticResource TextDim}"
                           IsVisible="{Binding HasConfidenceNote}"
                           Text="{Binding ConfidenceNote}"/>
            </StackPanel>
        </Border>

        <!-- ══ Working / quiet / broken ═════════════════════════════════════
             One sentence, never a blank pane and never a spinner. All three
             states point at the rail, because the library is one click away and
             a feed that cannot answer must not read as an app that cannot. -->
        <Border Grid.Row="1"
                Padding="32"
                IsVisible="{Binding ShowMessage}">
            <StackPanel MaxWidth="480" Spacing="18"
                        HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                <TextBlock Classes="body-l"
                           TextWrapping="Wrap"
                           TextAlignment="Center"
                           LineHeight="22"
                           Foreground="{StaticResource TextDim}"
                           Text="{Binding Message}"/>
                <!-- Only a FAILED pass offers this. A quiet feed is not
                     something to try again at, and a button saying so would be
                     the app arguing with its own answer. -->
                <Button Classes="act quiet"
                        Content="Try again"
                        HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                        IsVisible="{Binding CanRetry}"
                        Command="{Binding LoadCommand}"/>
            </StackPanel>
        </Border>

        <!-- ══ The shelves ══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <ScrollViewer Grid.Row="1"
                      Name="Page"
                      IsVisible="{Binding ShowShelves}"
                      HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                      VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
            <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Shelves}" Margin="0,0,0,28">
                <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                    <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:FeedShelfViewModel">
                        <StackPanel Margin="0,22,0,0">

                            <StackPanel Margin="24,0,24,12" Spacing="3">
                                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="10">
                                    <TextBlock Classes="shelf" Text="{Binding Title}"/>

                                    <!-- How many are on the rail, beside the
                                         name rather than out at the pane's far
                                         edge: a count belongs to the thing it
                                         counts, and a number a thousand pixels
                                         away from its noun is an orphan.

                                         This is what makes a SHORT shelf read as
                                         finished rather than as half-loaded —
                                         six cards saying 6 is an answer, three
                                         cards saying 10 is an invitation to
                                         scroll. -->
                                    <TextBlock Classes="data-s"
                                               VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                               Text="{Binding CountText}"/>
                                </StackPanel>
                                    <!-- The shelf's own pitch, in the engine's
                                         words. It is the sentence the cards
                                         under it are all instances of. -->
                                <TextBlock Classes="body"
                                           MaxWidth="720"
                                           HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                                           TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                           LineHeight="19"
                                           Foreground="{StaticResource TextDim}"
                                           Text="{Binding Blurb}"/>
                            </StackPanel>

                            <ScrollViewer Classes="rail"
                                          HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                                          VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled">
                                <ItemsControl Name="RailItems"
                                              ItemsSource="{Binding Cards}"
                                              Margin="24,0,24,4">
                                    <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                                        <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                                            <!-- Horizontal, so every card in one
                                                 rail is arranged to the tallest
                                                 card's height and the action row
                                                 lands on one line across the
                                                 shelf. A rail whose longest
                                                 reason is 256 characters is
                                                 simply a taller rail. -->
                                            <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="14"/>
                                        </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                                    </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                                    <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                                        <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:FeedCardViewModel">
                                            <views:FeedCardView/>
                                        </DataTemplate>
                                    </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                                </ItemsControl>
                            </ScrollViewer>

                        </StackPanel>
                    </DataTemplate>
                </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
            </ItemsControl>
        </ScrollViewer>

    </Grid>

</UserControl>
